{
  "$schema_note": "RECEIPTS.md \u00a77.1 receipt-canonical-bytes.json. Authoritative home is interlace-spec/0.2.0/test-vectors/ in cloister; this is notme's pinned copy. Both implementations MUST reproduce expected_canonical_cbor_hex byte-equal from `input`.",
  "note": "Generated by encoding each `input` with BOTH cloister's canonicalCbor(commitmentCborMap(...)) and notme's encodeCanonicalMap(...), and asserting the two agree byte-for-byte. All 12 vectors agreed at generation time. Pinning the INPUTS (not only the output bytes) is the point: if either side's commitment shape changes \u2014 a field added, order moved, an integer head widened \u2014 the side that made the change fails its own suite, instead of the other side failing at integration. A one-sided pin lets the party who can break the contract be the only one who cannot detect breaking it.",
  "landmines_covered": [
    "timestamp_ms at uint8/uint16/uint32/uint64 head boundaries (23/24, 255/256, 65535/65536, 2^32-1/2^32)",
    "body_hash with high-bit-set first byte (0xff) \u2014 byte-string encoding",
    "headers_hash = SHA-256 of the canonical empty map (0xa0)",
    "status at the 2xx upper bound (299)"
  ],
